== $K_0$ of maximal orders <sec:k0-of-maximal-orders>

We begin by defining the group $K_0 (Lambda)$ for an arbitrary ring $Lambda$.
Let $P(Lambda)$ denote the category of all finitely generated projective left
$Lambda$-modules. Let $bold(F)$ be the free abelian group generated by symbols
$(M)$, one for each isomorphism class of objects $M in P(Lambda)$. Let
$bold(F)_0$ be the subgroup of $bold(F)$ generated by all expressions
$(L dotplus M) - (L) - (M)$, where $L, M in P(Lambda)$. Now set
$K_0 (Lambda) = bold(F) slash bold(F)_0$. Then $K_0 (Lambda)$ is an abelian
additive group, called the _Grothendieck group_#idx("Grothendieck group") of the
category $P(Lambda)$. For $(M) in bold(F)$, let $[M]$ denote its image in
$bold(F) slash bold(F)_0$. Then every element of $K_0 (Lambda)$ is expressible
as a difference $[M] - [N]$, with $M, N in P(Lambda)$. For any
$L, M in P(Lambda)$, we have
$
  [L dotplus M] = [L] + [M] quad "in" K_0 (Lambda).
$ <eq:k0-sum-relation>

Given $M$, $N in P(Lambda)$, we call $M$ _stably isomorphic_#idx(
  "stable isomorphism",
) to $N$ if $M dotplus Lambda^((r)) tilde.equiv N dotplus Lambda^((r))$ for some
integer $r$. By Exercise~@exc:k0-stable-isomorphism, $[M] = [N]$ in
$K_0 (Lambda)$ if and only if $M$ is stably isomorphic to $N$. Hence we may use
the symbol $[M]$ to denote both an element of $K_0 (Lambda)$ and the stable
isomorphism class of $M$.

Each ring $Lambda$ gives rise to an abelian group $K_0 (Lambda)$. Further, each
homomorphism of rings $phi : Lambda -> Lambda'$ gives rise to a homomorphism
$phi_* : K_0 (Lambda) -> K_0 (Lambda')$ of additive groups. The map $phi_*$ is
defined by setting
$
  phi_* ([M] - [N]) = [Lambda' times.o_Lambda M] - [Lambda' times.o_Lambda N],
  quad [M] - [N] in K_0 (Lambda).
$
In the expression $Lambda' times.o_Lambda M$, the ring $Lambda'$ is made into a
right $Lambda$-module by means of $phi$. It is easily verified that $phi_*$ is
well defined. In the language of category theory, we may view $K_0$ as a
(covariant) functor from the category of rings to the category of abelian
groups.

It follows at once from this fact that if $dsum_(i=1)^n Lambda_i$ is a direct
sum of rings ("direct product", in category language), then there is a natural
isomorphism
#source(324)
$
  K_0 (dsum_(i=1)^n Lambda_i) tilde.equiv dsum_(i=1)^n K_0 (Lambda_i).
$ <eq:k0-direct-sum>
We also note that if the rings $Gamma$ and $Lambda$ are Morita equivalent (see
§~@sec:morita-correspondence), then the equivalence of categories
$lmodule(Lambda, cal(M)) -> lmodule(Gamma, cal(M))$ induces an equivalence
$P(Lambda) -> P(Gamma)$. This latter equivalence then gives rise to an
isomorphism $K_0 (Lambda) tilde.equiv K_0 (Gamma)$. We shall use this fact in
our discussion below.

We are now ready to investigate $K_0$ of a maximal order. By
@th:maximal-orders-components, a maximal order in a separable algebra decomposes
as a ring direct sum of maximal orders in central simple algebras. In view of
@eq:k0-direct-sum, it therefore suffices to calculate $K_0 (Lambda)$ for the
case where $Lambda$ is a maximal $R$-order in a central simple $K$-algebra. The
aim of this section is to prove that
$
  K_0 (Lambda) tilde.equiv bold(Z) dotplus Cl Lambda,
$
where $Cl Lambda$ is the ideal class group of $Lambda$ defined in
§~@sec:ideal-class-groups. We showed in @th:swan-class-group that $Cl Lambda$ is
isomorphic to the ray class group $Cl_A R$ when $K$ is an algebraic number
field, or when $K$ is a function field and $A = eichler(R)$. Hence in these
cases the structure of $K_0 (Lambda)$ is known explicitly.

Our main theorem, which holds whether or not $K$ is a global field, is as
follows:

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, and let $Lambda$ be a
  maximal $R$-order in the central simple $K$-algebra $A$. There is an exact
  sequence of additive groups
  $
    0 -> Cl Lambda stretch(->)^mu K_0 (Lambda) stretch(->)^psi K_0 (A) -> 0.
  $ <eq:k0-exact-sequence>
  Furthermore,
  $
    K_0 (A) tilde.equiv bold(Z), quad
    K_0 (Lambda) tilde.equiv bold(Z) dotplus Cl Lambda.
  $ <eq:k0-structure>
] <th:k0-maximal-order>

#proof[
  The inclusion $Lambda -> A$ induces $psi : K_0 (Lambda) -> K_0 (A)$, where
  $
    psi[M] = [A times.o_Lambda M] = [K times.o_R Lambda times.o_Lambda M]
    = [K times.o_R M]
  $
  for each $M in P(Lambda)$. Since $M$ is a $Lambda$-lattice, we may identify
  $K times.o_R M$ with $K M$.

  In order to show that $psi$ is epic, it suffices to prove that for each
  finitely generated left $A$-module $W$, there exists an $M in P(Lambda)$ such
  that $K M tilde.equiv W$. By Exercise~@exc:lattice-generating-localization or
  Exercise~@exc:jordan-zassenhaus-quotients, there exists a left
  $Lambda$-lattice $M$ in $W$ such that $K M = W$. But then $M in P(Lambda)$,
  since by @cor:global-lattices-projective every $Lambda$-lattice is
  $Lambda$-projective. Therefore $psi$ is an epimorphism.

  The ideal class group $Cl Lambda$ consists of all stable isomorphism classes
  of left $Lambda$-ideals in $A$, where a "left $Lambda$-ideal in $A$" means a
  left $Lambda$-lattice $M$ in $A$ such that $K M = A$. Addition in $Cl Lambda$
  is given by
  $
    [M] + [M'] = [M''] quad "if" quad
    M dotplus M' tilde.equiv Lambda dotplus M'',
  $
  #source(325)where the $M$'s are left $Lambda$-ideals in $A$. We define
  $mu : Cl Lambda -> K_0 (Lambda)$ by setting
  $
    mu[M] = [Lambda] - [M], quad [M] in Cl Lambda.
  $
  Since the element $[M] in K_0 (Lambda)$ depends only on the stable isomorphism
  class of $M$, it is clear that $mu$ is monic. Furthermore,
  $
    M dotplus M' tilde.equiv Lambda dotplus M''
    & => [M] + [M'] = [Lambda] + [M''] quad "in" quad K_0 (Lambda) \
    & => [Lambda] - [M] + [Lambda] - [M'] = [Lambda] - [M''] \
    & => mu[M] + mu[M'] = mu[M''] = mu{[M] + [M']}.
  $
  Therefore $mu$ is a homomorphism of groups.

  For $[M] in Cl Lambda$, we have
  $
    psi mu[M] = [A] - [A] = 0 quad "in" quad K_0 (A),
  $
  so $psi mu = 0$. To prove exactness of the sequence @eq:k0-exact-sequence, it
  remains to verify that $ker psi subset im mu$. Each $x in K_0 (Lambda)$ may be
  written as $x = [M] - [N]$, with $M, N in P(Lambda)$. If we choose
  $M' in P(Lambda)$ so that $M dotplus M' tilde.equiv Lambda^((r))$ for some
  $r$, then
  $
    x = [M dotplus M'] - [N dotplus M'] = [Lambda^((r))] - [L]
  $
  for some $L in P(Lambda)$. Suppose now that $psi(x) = 0$; then
  $[K L] = [A^((r))]$ in $K_0 (A)$. Since $A$ is a simple artinian ring, it
  follows from Exercise~@exc:k0-semisimple that $K L tilde.equiv A^((r))$ as
  left $A$-modules. Hence by @th:lattice-free-plus-ideal there exists a left
  $Lambda$-ideal $L_0$ in $A$ such that
  $L tilde.equiv Lambda^((r-1)) dotplus L_0$. Therefore
  $
    x = [Lambda^((r))] - [Lambda^((r-1)) dotplus L_0] = [Lambda] - [L_0]
    = mu[L_0].
  $
  This completes the proof that @eq:k0-exact-sequence is exact.

  Since $A$ is a simple artinian ring, we have $K_0 (A) tilde.equiv bold(Z)$ by
  Exercise~@exc:k0-semisimple. Therefore the sequence @eq:k0-exact-sequence
  splits as a sequence of $bold(Z)$-modules. This shows that
  $K_0 (Lambda) tilde.equiv bold(Z) dotplus Cl Lambda$, and completes the proof
  of the theorem.
]

Let us now compare the sequence @eq:k0-exact-sequence with that associated with
an order $Gamma$ which is Morita equivalent to $Lambda$. For simplicity, we
treat only the case where $Gamma tilde.equiv M_n (Lambda)$.

#theorem[
  Keep the notation of @th:k0-maximal-order, and let $n >= 1$. Set
  $
    B & = Hom_A (V, V) tilde.equiv M_n (A), & quad "where" quad & V = A^((n)), \
    Gamma & = Hom_Lambda (L, L) tilde.equiv M_n (Lambda),
    & quad "where" quad & L = Lambda^((n)),
  $
  and where the bimodule structures are given by $lmodule(B, V)_A$,
  $lmodule(Gamma, L)_Lambda$. Then there is a commutative diagram with exact
  rows:
  #source(326)
  $
    #k0-morita-ladder
  $ <eq:k0-morita-diagram>
  where the map $phi$ is given by
  $
    phi[M] = [L times.o_Lambda (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)], quad
    [M] in Cl Lambda.
  $
  Each vertical map in @eq:k0-morita-diagram is an isomorphism.
] <th:k0-matrix-order>

#proof[
  Since $V_A$ is a progenerator for $cal(M)_A$, the ring $B$ is Morita
  equivalent to $A$, and there is an equivalence of categories
  $
    V times.o_A dot : lmodule(A, cal(M)) -> lmodule(B, cal(M)).
  $
  Likewise, there is an equivalence
  $
    L times.o_Lambda dot : lmodule(Lambda, cal(M)) -> lmodule(Gamma, cal(M)).
  $
  The second and third vertical maps in @eq:k0-morita-diagram are just the
  isomorphisms induced by these category equivalences. The right hand square in
  @eq:k0-morita-diagram is commutative, since both $psi$ and $psi'$ are induced
  by the functor $K times.o_R dot$.

  It follows from the Morita equivalence of $Gamma$ and $Lambda$ that there is a
  left $Gamma$-isomorphism
  $
    rho : Gamma tilde.equiv L times.o_Lambda Lambda^((n)),
  $ <eq:matrix-order-tensor-iso>
  where $lmodule(Gamma, L)_Lambda$ is a bimodule, and $Lambda^((n))$ is viewed
  as left $Lambda$-module. For $M$ a left ideal in $Lambda$, we may therefore
  view $L times.o_Lambda (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)$ as a left ideal in $Gamma$.
  This readily implies that the stable isomorphism class of $M$ uniquely
  determines that of $L times.o_Lambda (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)$, and so $phi$
  is well defined on $Cl Lambda$.

  We shall show that the left hand square in @eq:k0-morita-diagram is
  commutative. This implies at once that $phi$ is an isomorphism, as claimed. To
  prove commutativity, we start with an arbitrary $[M] in Cl Lambda$, and make
  the following calculation in $K_0 (Gamma)$. (In this calculation, we use
  @eq:matrix-order-tensor-iso, and write $times.o$ in place of $times.o_Lambda$
  for brevity.) We have
  $
    mu' phi[M] & = [Gamma] - [L times.o (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)] \
               & = [L times.o Lambda^((n))] - [L times.o Lambda^((n-1))]
                 - [L times.o M] \
               & = [L times.o Lambda] - [L times.o M] = (L times.o dot) mu[M].
  $
  This shows that the left hand square in @eq:k0-morita-diagram is commutative,
  and completes the proof of the theorem.
]

#remark(kind: "Remarks")[
  + Keeping the above notation, let $S$ be a simple left $A$-module. Then $[S]$
    is a free $bold(Z)$-generator for $K_0 (A)$, and there is an isomorphism
    $r_A : K_0 (A) tilde.equiv bold(Z)$, obtained by setting $r_A [S] = 1$.
    Since $V times.o_A S$ is a simple #source(327)left $B$-module, it follows at
    once that the following diagram commutes:
    $
      #rank-square
    $
  + Suppose that the hypotheses of @th:swan-class-group hold, and keep the above
    notation. The reduced norm maps induce isomorphisms
    $
      nu : Cl Lambda tilde.equiv Cl_A R, quad nu' : Cl Gamma tilde.equiv Cl_B R,
    $
    and of course $Cl_A R = Cl_B R$. We claim that the diagram
    $
      #norm-square
    $ <eq:class-group-norm-square>
    is commutative. Let $[M] in Cl Lambda$, and assume without loss of
    generality that $M subset Lambda$. We need only show that
    $
      nr_(A slash K) M = nr_(B slash K) {L times.o (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)},
    $ <eq:reduced-norm-matrix-ideal>
    where $times.o$ means $times.o_Lambda$. If $(A : K) = t^2$, then
    $(B : K) = t^2 n^2$, so
    $
      N_(A slash K) = (nr_(A slash K))^t, quad
      N_(B slash K) = (nr_(B slash K))^(t n).
    $
    Thus @eq:reduced-norm-matrix-ideal holds if and only if
    $
      {N_(A slash K) M}^n
      = N_(B slash K) {L times.o (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)}.
    $
    Using @eq:matrix-order-tensor-iso, the right hand expression equals
    $
      ord_R frac(L times.o Lambda^((n)), L times.o (Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M))
      & = {ord_R frac(Lambda^((n)), Lambda^((n-1)) dotplus M)}^n \
      & = {ord_R frac(Lambda, M)}^n = {N_(A slash K) M}^n.
    $
    This completes the proof that the diagram @eq:class-group-norm-square is
    commutative.
] <rem:k0-morita-compatibility>

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Exercises]

In Exercises~@exc:k0-stable-isomorphism and~@exc:k0-differences, $Lambda$ is an
arbitrary ring.

#exercise[
  Let $X, Y in P(Lambda)$. Show that $[X] = [Y]$ in $K_0 (Lambda)$ if and only
  if $X$ is stably isomorphic to $Y$. #hint[@exc:k0-stable-isomorphism][If
    $X dotplus Lambda^((r)) tilde.equiv Y dotplus Lambda^((r))$, then
    $[X] + r[Lambda] = [Y] + r[Lambda]$ in $K_0 (Lambda)$, so $[X] = [Y]$.
    Conversely, if $[X] = [Y]$ in $K_0 (Lambda)$, then $(X) - (Y)$ lies in the
    subgroup $bold(F)_0$ of $bold(F)$. Hence there exist $L$'s, $M$'s in
    $P(Lambda)$ such that in $bold(F)$,
    #source(328)
    $
      (X) - (Y) = sum {(L_i dotplus L'_i) - (L_i) - (L'_i)}
      + sum {(M_j) + (M'_j) - (M_j dotplus M'_j)}.
    $
    Therefore
    $
      X dotplus dsum L_i dotplus dsum L'_i dotplus dsum (M_j dotplus M'_j)
      tilde.equiv Y dotplus dsum (L_i dotplus L'_i) dotplus dsum M_j dotplus
      dsum M'_j.
    $
    Choose $N in P(Lambda)$ so that
    $
      N dotplus dsum L_i dotplus dsum L'_i dotplus dsum M_j dotplus dsum M'_j
      tilde.equiv Lambda^((r))
    $
    for some $r$. Then
    $X dotplus Lambda^((r)) tilde.equiv Y dotplus Lambda^((r))$, as desired.]
  <hint:k0-stable-isomorphism>
] <exc:k0-stable-isomorphism>

#exercise[
  Let $X_i, Y_i in P(Lambda)$. Show that $[X_1] - [X_2] = [Y_1] - [Y_2]$ in
  $K_0 (Lambda)$ if and only if $X_1 dotplus Y_2$ is stably isomorphic to
  $X_2 dotplus Y_1$.
] <exc:k0-differences>

#exercise[
  Let $A$ be a semisimple artinian ring with $t$ simple components. Show that
  + $P(A)$ consists of all finitely generated left $A$-modules.
  + For $X, Y in P(A)$, we have $[X] = [Y]$ in $K_0 (A)$ if and only if
    $X tilde.equiv Y$.
  + $K_0 (A) tilde.equiv bold(Z)^((t))$. In fact, if $S_1, dots, S_t$ are a full
    set of non-isomorphic simple left $A$-modules, show that
    $[S_1], dots, [S_t]$ form a free $bold(Z)$-basis for $K_0 (A)$.
] <exc:k0-semisimple>

In Exercises~@exc:lattices-progenerators–@exc:k0-morita-analogues, let $R$ be a
Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, and let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order
in a central simple $K$-algebra $A$.

#exercise[
  Show that _every_ nonzero left $Lambda$-lattice is a progenerator of the
  category $lmodule(Lambda, cal(M))$.
] <exc:lattices-progenerators>

#exercise[
  Show that @th:k0-matrix-order can be generalized, as follows: let $L$ be any
  nonzero right $Lambda$-lattice, and set
  $
    Gamma = Hom_Lambda (L, L), quad
    L^* = Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Gamma, L)_Lambda, Lambda_Lambda),
  $
  with bimodule structures $lmodule(Gamma, L)_Lambda$,
  $lmodule(Lambda, (L^*))_Gamma$. Let $V = K L$, $B = K Gamma$. Show that the
  diagram @eq:k0-morita-diagram is commutative, provided we define $phi[M]$ for
  $[M] in Cl Lambda$ as follows:
  $
    phi[M] = [L times.o_Lambda tilde(M)],
  $
  where $tilde(M)$ is a left $Lambda$-lattice such that
  $M dotplus L^* tilde.equiv Lambda dotplus tilde(M)$.
  #hint[@exc:k0-morita-generalized][The equivalence
    $L times.o_Lambda dot : lmodule(Lambda, cal(M)) -> lmodule(Gamma, cal(M))$
    carries $L^*$ onto $Gamma$. This fact is crucial in proving the
    commutativity of the left hand square in @eq:k0-morita-diagram.]
  <hint:k0-morita-generalized>
] <exc:k0-morita-generalized>

#exercise[
  Keep the notation of the preceding exercise. Show that the analogues of
  @rem:k0-morita-compatibility[(i)] and @rem:k0-morita-compatibility[(ii)]
  remain true.
] <exc:k0-morita-analogues>
